= Solution
{parent=a}

There are $\binom72=21$ equally likely unordered pairs, and $3\cdot4=12$ contain one ball of each colour. Thus the <hypergeometric distribution> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\text{different colours})=\frac{12}{21}=\frac47.}
$$

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $I_1,I_2$ be <indicator random variables> for the two draws being red. Each draw has red <probability> $3/7$, even though the draws are dependent. By <linearity of expectation>, the number $R=I_1+I_2$ satisfies
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[R]=\frac37+\frac37=\frac67.}
$$

= Section B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Substituting $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$ into the <circle> equation gives $r^2-2r\cos\theta=0$. Thus its nonzero radial intersection is $r_Q=2\cos\theta$, with the origin also on the <circle>. The <tangent to a circle> at the rightmost point is $x=2$, or $r_R\cos\theta=2$.

Take rays with $-\pi/2<\theta<\pi/2$. The difference of the two radial distances gives the <cissoid of Diocles>:
$$
\boxed{r_P=2\sec\theta-2\cos\theta=\frac{2\sin^2\theta}{\cos\theta}.}
$$
Multiplying by $\cos\theta$ gives $x=2\sin^2\theta=2y^2/(x^2+y^2)$, hence the Cartesian equation $y^2(2-x)=x^3$. A convenient <parametric curve>, with $t=\tan\theta$, is
$$
x=\frac{2t^2}{1+t^2},\qquad y=\frac{2t^3}{1+t^2},\qquad -\infty<t<\infty.
$$
The real <curve> has $0\leq x<2$, a <cusp> at the origin, symmetry about the $x$ axis, and the vertical <asymptote> $x=2$. Near the origin, $x\sim2t^2$ and $y\sim2t^3$, so its cusp has horizontal tangent direction. As $|t|\to\infty$, $x\to2^-$ while $|y|\to\infty$. The circle and curve meet away from the origin at $(1,\pm1)$.

\Image[paper-2-cissoid.png]
{title=The cissoid has a cusp at the origin and approaches the tangent line}
{description=An original plot of the derived parametric formula, with the unit <circle> centred at $(1,0)$ and its <tangent line> $x=2$.}

= 2
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <tangent half-angle substitution> $t=\tan(x/2)$ gives $\sin x=2t/(1+t^2)$ and $dx=2\,dt/(1+t^2)$. The endpoints $x=0,\pi/2$ become $t=0,1$, respectively. Hence
$$
\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{dx}{2+\sin x}=\int_0^1\frac{2\,dt}{2(1+t^2)+2t}=\int_0^1\frac{dt}{t^2+t+1}.
$$
<Completing the square>, $t^2+t+1=(t+1/2)^2+3/4$, then gives
$$
\int_0^1\frac{dt}{t^2+t+1}=\frac2{\sqrt3}\left[\arctan\frac{2t+1}{\sqrt3}\right]_0^1=\frac2{\sqrt3}\left(\frac\pi3-\frac\pi6\right)=\boxed{\frac\pi{3\sqrt3}}.
$$

= 3
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Normalization of a probability distribution> and the <geometric series> give $1=A/(1-\rho)$, so $A=1-\rho$. For a nonnegative <integer> $n$, the strict tail starts at $n+1$:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N>n)=\sum_{j=n+1}^\infty(1-\rho)\rho^j=\rho^{n+1}.}
$$
For nonnegative <integers> $n>m$, the <event> $\{N>n\}$ is contained in $\{N>m\}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N>n\mid N>m)=\frac{\rho^{n+1}}{\rho^{m+1}}=\rho^{n-m}.}
$$
This is the <memorylessness of the geometric distribution> with the zero-based counting convention. Keeping strict versus non-strict inequalities explicit is essential: $N-m-1$, conditional on $N>m$, has the original distribution of $N$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $\lambda=\ln(\rho^{-1})>0$. The <probability density function> normalizes when $1=B\int_0^\infty e^{-\lambda x}dx=B/\lambda$, so $B=\lambda$. Its <survival function> is $e^{-\lambda x}$ for $x\geq0$; hence, for $x>y\geq0$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X>x\mid X>y)=e^{-\lambda(x-y)}.}
$$
Differentiating this conditional tail gives
$$
\boxed{f_{X\mid X>y}(x)=\begin{cases}\lambda e^{-\lambda(x-y)},&x>y,\\0,&x<y.\end{cases}}
$$
The value assigned at the single endpoint does not affect any <probability>. The <memorylessness of the exponential distribution> says that $X-y$, conditional on $X>y$, is again an <exponential distribution> of rate $\lambda$. Its first two <moments> are $1/\lambda$ and $2/\lambda^2$, found by <integration by parts>, so translation leaves the <variance> unchanged:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(X\mid X>y)=\frac1{\lambda^2}=\frac1{[\ln(\rho^{-1})]^2}.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <characteristic equation> for the <homogeneous solution> is $(r+2)^2=0$, giving $(C_1+C_2x)e^{-2x}$. For a <particular solution>, substitute $Ae^{2x}$: the left side becomes $16Ae^{2x}$, so $A=1/16$. The <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y=(C_1+C_2x)e^{-2x}+\frac1{16}e^{2x}.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <characteristic equation> has roots $-3\pm4i$, giving the <homogeneous solution> $e^{-3x}(A\cos4x+B\sin4x)$. For the sinusoidal <forcing term>, $y_p=\sin5x$ is a <particular solution>, since its second <derivative> cancels $25y_p$ and $6y_p'=30\cos5x$. The zero initial displacement gives $A=0$; zero initial <derivative> gives $4B+5=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sin5x-\frac54e^{-3x}\sin4x.}
$$
The <transient response> decays as $e^{-3x}$, leaving the <steady-state response> $\sin5x$. Near zero, $y(0)=y'(0)=0$ and $y''(0)=30$, so the sketch starts with $y\sim15x^2$ and a horizontal <tangent line>.

\Image[paper-2-driven-oscillation.png]
{title=The decaying transient brings the initially stationary solution to a sinusoidal response}
{description=Solid: the complete solution. Dashed: the surviving $\sin5x$ term. The lower panel resolves the exponentially decaying difference.}

= 5
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

If $u\,dx+v\,dy=dF$ with continuous second <partial derivatives> of $F$, then $u=F_x$ and $v=F_y$. The <symmetry of second derivatives> gives the necessary condition for an <exact differential form>:
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right)_x=\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\right)_y.}
$$
This condition is also locally sufficient on a small rectangle for continuously <differentiable> coefficients; global sufficiency additionally needs suitable topology of the domain.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Holding $y$ fixed, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial u}\right)_y\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}\right)_y=\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\right)_y,}
$$
where the local change of variable from $x$ to $u$ is valid.

For the <internal energy> $U(S,V)$, its <exact differential> identifies $T=U_S$ and $p=-U_V$. The <Maxwell relation> from equality of mixed <partial derivatives> is $T_V|_S=-p_S|_V$. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, the <constant-volume heat capacity> satisfies
$$
C_V=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}\right)_V=T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial T}\right)_V,
$$
so $T_S|_V=T/C_V$, assuming nonzero $C_V$ and a locally invertible relation. Applying the <chain rule> to $p(T,V)$ gives the <isentropic temperature-volume derivative>
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial V}\right)_S=-\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial S}\right)_V=-\frac{T}{C_V}\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V.}
$$
For the stated one-mole <ideal gas>, the <equation of state> gives $p_T|_V=R/V$ and $C_V=3R/2$, so the right side is $-2T/(3V)$. Along an <isentropic process>, $p\propto V^{-5/3}$ and $T=pV/R\propto V^{-2/3}$, giving the same left side:
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial V}\right)_S=-\frac{2T}{3V}.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Section B}
