= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Taking the <curl> component by component gives
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf F=\bigl(\partial_yx-\partial_z(x^2+2y),\ \partial_z(2xy+z)-\partial_xx,\ \partial_x(x^2+2y)-\partial_y(2xy+z)\bigr)=\boxed{\mathbf0}.
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Integrating $\psi_x=-(2xy+z)$ with respect to $x$ gives $\psi=-x^2y-xz+h(y,z)$. Matching $\psi_y=-(x^2+2y)$ requires $h=-y^2+j(z)$, and matching $\psi_z=-x$ requires $j$ constant. A <scalar potential> with the requested sign is
$$
\boxed{\psi=-x^2y-xz-y^2+C.}
$$
Thus the <force field> is a <conservative vector field> on all of $\mathbb R^3$.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Parametrize the <straight line> by $\mathbf x(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$. The <line integral> is
$$
\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf x=\int_0^1\bigl[(2t^2+t)+(t^2+2t)+t\bigr]dt=\int_0^1(3t^2+4t)dt=\boxed{3}.
$$
The change in <scalar potential> is $\psi(1,1,1)-\psi(0,0,0)=-3$, so the answer is $-\Delta\psi$, as required by the <fundamental theorem for line integrals> for $\mathbf F=-\nabla\psi$.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For positive semiaxes $a,b$, the <cross product> of the two parametric <tangent vectors> gives the outward <oriented surface element of an ellipsoid of revolution>:
$$
\mathbf x_\theta\times\mathbf x_\phi=(ab\sin^2\theta\cos\phi,\ ab\sin^2\theta\sin\phi,\ b^2\sin\theta\cos\theta).
$$
Substituting the parametrization into the <vector field> and taking its <dot product> with this element gives
$$
\mathbf G\cdot(\mathbf x_\theta\times\mathbf x_\phi)=a^3b^2\sin^3\theta\cos^2\theta\cos^2\phi+ab^4\sin^5\theta\cos\phi\sin^3\phi+a^3b^2\sin\theta\cos^4\theta.
$$
The middle term has zero <integral> over $0\leq\phi\leq2\pi$. In the other terms, $\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^2\phi\,d\phi=\pi$, $\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\cos^2\theta\,d\theta=4/15$, and $\int_0^\pi\sin\theta\cos^4\theta\,d\theta=2/5$. The directly evaluated <flux> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_S\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf S=a^3b^2\left(\frac{4\pi}{15}+\frac{4\pi}{5}\right)=\frac{16\pi a^3b^2}{15}.}
$$

