= Solution
{parent=i}

**True.** Multiplicativity of the <determinant> and the identity $\det(B^{-1})=1/\det B$ give $\det(B^{-1}AB)=\det(B^{-1})\det A\det B=\det A$. This is invariance under <matrix similarity>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** The <cyclic property of the trace> permits $ABC\mapsto BCA$, but not an arbitrary interchange. Even with $A$ and $B$ both <invertible matrices>, take
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&2\end{pmatrix},\quad B=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad C=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Then $\operatorname{Tr}(ABC)=1$ and $\operatorname{Tr}(BAC)=2$, providing a counterexample.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** Distribute the <matrix product> while retaining its order:
$$
A^{-1}(A+B)B^{-1}=A^{-1}AB^{-1}+A^{-1}BB^{-1}=B^{-1}+A^{-1}=A^{-1}+B^{-1}.
$$
Only addition is commuted; no commutation of $A$ and $B$ is assumed.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <permutation matrix> cycles the three coordinate <vectors>, so three applications give $C^3=I$. Consequently $C^2C=CC^2=I$, proving $C^{-1}=C^2$. Since $16=5\cdot3+1$,
$$
\boxed{C^{16}=C=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&1\\1&0&0\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Expansion of the <determinant> gives $\det M=-\mu-\mu=-2\mu$. The <matrix inverse> exists precisely when
$$
\boxed{\mu\ne0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $\mu\ne0$, the <adjugate matrix> formula, or elimination on the three unit right-hand sides, gives
$$
\boxed{M^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}\frac1{2\mu}&\frac12&-\frac1{2\mu}\\\frac12&-\frac\mu2&\frac12\\-\frac1{2\mu}&\frac12&\frac1{2\mu}\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication by $M$ gives the <identity matrix>, checking all signs and coefficients.

= d
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Subtracting the first and third equations gives $\mu(x-z)=0$. Since $\mu\ne0$, $x=z$, and the second equation gives $x=z=1$. The first then gives $y=1-\mu$, so the unique solution of the <system of linear equations> is
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(1,1-\mu,1).}
$$

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $\mu=0$, the first and third equations both reduce to $y=1$, while the second gives $x+z=2$. All solutions form the <straight line>
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)+t(1,0,-1),\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The limit of the nonzero-$\mu$ solution selects only the point $(1,1,1)$ on this whole line; setting a parameter to its singular value can enlarge the solution set.

= 8
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Use the real <Fourier series> convention $f(x)\sim a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}[a_n\cos(n\pi x)+b_n\sin(n\pi x)]$ for period two. The <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
a_0=\int_0^1 2x\,dx=1,\quad a_n=\int_0^1 2x\cos(n\pi x)\,dx=\frac{2[(-1)^n-1]}{n^2\pi^2},\quad b_n=\int_0^1 2x\sin(n\pi x)\,dx=\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\pi}.
$$
The last two <integrals> follow by <integration by parts>. Thus the <Fourier series of a half-wave rectified ramp> is
$$
f(x)\sim\frac12-\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{r=0}^\infty\frac{\cos((2r+1)\pi x)}{(2r+1)^2}+\frac2\pi\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n\sin(n\pi x).
$$
On $(-1,1)$, $f(x)=|x|+x$. Its <even> part is $f_e(x)=|x|$ and its <odd> part is $f_o(x)=x$, so the cosine and sine terms separate:
$$
\boxed{f_e(x)=\frac12-\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{r=0}^\infty\frac{\cos((2r+1)\pi x)}{(2r+1)^2},\qquad f_o(x)=\frac2\pi\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n\sin(n\pi x).}
$$
At a jump, the <Fourier series> converges to the average of the one-sided limits. Thus the first series for $f$ takes the value $1$ at odd integers, while the <Fourier series of a periodic sawtooth> for $f_o$ takes the value $0$ there. The even function has a continuous periodic extension.

At $x=0$, the formula for $f_e$ gives $0=1/2-(4/\pi^2)\sum_{r\geq0}(2r+1)^{-2}$. At $x=1/2$, the formula for $f_o$ has only odd-index terms and gives $1/2=(2/\pi)\sum_{r\geq0}(-1)^r/(2r+1)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\sum_{r=0}^\infty\frac1{(2r+1)^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{8},\qquad\sum_{r=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^r}{2r+1}=\frac\pi4.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Set $g(x,y)=y-(x-2)^2$. The <gradient> of $g$ never vanishes, so the <Lagrange multiplier> equations $\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g$ apply at a constrained <stationary point>. They are $4x=-2\lambda(x-2)$, $2y=\lambda$, and $y=(x-2)^2$. Eliminating $\lambda,y$ gives
$$
x+(x-2)^3=0.
$$
With $t=x-2$, this becomes $t^3+t+2=(t+1)(t^2-t+2)=0$. The quadratic factor is $(t-1/2)^2+7/4>0$, so the only real possibility is $t=-1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(1,1),\qquad f=3,\qquad\lambda=2.}
$$
The <level curve> through this point is the <ellipse> $2x^2+y^2=3$, meeting the coordinate axes at $(\pm\sqrt{3/2},0)$ and $(0,\pm\sqrt3)$. Its <tangent line> at $(1,1)$ has <slope> $-2x/y=-2$, equal to the <derivative> $2(x-2)$ of the constraint <parabola> there. The two <curves> are tangent.

\Image[paper-2-constrained-minimum.png]
{title=The smallest elliptical contour meeting the constraint is tangent at the minimum}
{description=The highlighted contour is $2x^2+y^2=3$ and the constraint is $y=(x-2)^2$. Its four axis intercepts and the unique contact point $(1,1)$ are marked.}

The <parabola> touches this <ellipse> from outside; every other point on the constraint lies on a larger contour. An exact check of this geometric argument is
$$
2x^2+(x-2)^4-3=(x-1)^2\bigl[(x-3)^2+4\bigr]\geq0,
$$
with equality only at $x=1$. Thus the point is the unique constrained <global minimum>.

= 10
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

Substitute $u(y,t)=Y(y)T(t)$ into the <diffusion equation> and divide on a region where both factors are nonzero. This gives $Y''/Y=T'/(\nu T)$. The two sides depend on different variables, so they equal a separation <constant> $a$:
$$
\boxed{Y''=aY,\qquad T'=\nu aT.}
$$
These identities then extend through the zeros of the separated factors. The zero <Dirichlet boundary conditions> require $Y(0)=Y(1)=0$. For a nontrivial solution, $a$ must be negative: $a=0$ gives only a vanishing linear function and $a>0$ gives only a vanishing hyperbolic-sine solution. Writing $a=-k^2$ gives $Y=A\sin(ky)$, and $k=n\pi$, $n=1,2,\ldots$. Consequently the separated modes of <free viscous decay between parallel plates> are $\sin(n\pi y)e^{-\nu n^2\pi^2t}$.

The first initial condition selects the lowest mode:
$$
\boxed{u(y,t)=\sin(\pi y)e^{-\nu\pi^2t}.}
$$
The <superposition principle> for a homogeneous <linear differential equation> states that any linear combination of solutions is again a solution, with the same combination of homogeneous <boundary conditions> and initial data. It therefore gives
$$
\boxed{u(y,t)=\sin(\pi y)e^{-\nu\pi^2t}+\frac1{10}\sin(3\pi y)e^{-9\nu\pi^2t}.}
$$
The higher spatial mode decays nine times as fast, so the <velocity> profile approaches a multiple of the first sine mode before vanishing altogether.
